<misterromdescription>
    <name>Raiden Fighters (Germany, SPI cartridge, pre-flashed)</name>
    <setname>rdft</setname>
    <rbf>SeibuSPI</rbf>
    <mameversion>0277</mameversion>
    <year>1996</year>
    <manufacturer>Seibu Kaihatsu</manufacturer>
    <players>2</players>
    <joystick>8</joystick>
    <rotation>vertical (cw)</rotation>
    <region>Germany</region>
    <category>Shooter</category>

    <!--
      THE SPI CARTRIDGE HAS NO SAMPLE ROM. Its YMF271 plays from two Intel
      E28F008SA flash chips that the game programs itself on first boot -- the
      several-minute "techno music" reflash. This MRA ships the flash
      PRE-PROGRAMMED, so the updater is skipped and the game boots straight in.

      The image is not a dump. It is derived from the cartridge's own sound
      ROMs, and built this way it is byte-identical to what the game writes
      (PLAN.md section 0, measured under MAME):

        flash[0x000000-0x000003] = maincpu[0x1FFFFC-0x1FFFFF]   region + build
        flash[0x000004-0x1A13B5] = gun_dogs_pcm.u0217[4..]
        flash[0x1A13B6-0x1D9642] = seibu_8.u0216[0..0x3828C]
        flash[0x1D9643-0x1FFFFF] = 0xFF

      The four header bytes are not a checksum. They are a verbatim copy of the
      last four bytes of the 386 program -- region byte plus build ID -- and
      they are the whole region lock: a stamp that does not match the program
      makes the game reflash. So this image is specific to THIS set's program
      ROMs, and a different region's cartridge needs its own.

      `sound01` is deliberately absent. That 10 MB window exists so the updater
      can read the samples out of the 386's address space; with the flash
      already programmed the 386 never touches it. Measured rather than
      assumed: a read tap over 0xA00000-0x13FFFFF counted 0 reads across 4800
      frames pre-flashed, against 97,901 by frame 2100 on a blank flash.
    -->
    <!--
      Part order is significant. rtl/rom_loader.sv walks this list with a fixed
      table -- selected by the mod byte below -- and performs MAME's
      ROM_LOAD24_* / ROM_LOAD32_* byte scatter in hardware, because MRA
      <interleave> cannot express the 3-byte period the Seibu "D + P" graphics
      ROM pairs need. Do not reorder or insert parts without updating the
      SXX2C table in rom_loader.sv to match. `make check-mra` checks them
      against each other and against MAME's driver.

      Layout produced in SDRAM (see rtl/spi_defs.vh):
        0x0000000  2 MB   386 program
        0x0200000  256 KB Z80 program -- RAM, NOT loaded here. The 386 fills it
                          through port 0x688 and releases the Z80 with 0x68C.
        0x0240000  192 KB text tiles
        0x0280000  2 MB   YMF271 samples: the pre-programmed flash image
        0x0480000  6 MB   background tiles
        0x0A80000  12 MB  sprites, three 4 MB plane-pair chunks
    -->

    <!--
      Index 1 is the core's config blob. Byte 0 is the mod byte: bit 0 selects
      the SXX2C part table and board wiring in the core. rdfts.mra leaves this
      at 0.

      Everything AFTER byte 0 is a list of {part index, codec id} pairs, which
      is how an MRA says "decode this part on the way into SDRAM" instead of
      copying it. Parts default to CODEC_RAW, so this file needs none.

        id  name             what it is
        --  ---------------  --------------------------------------------------
        0   CODEC_RAW        straight copy (the default)
        1   CODEC_BPE_DPCM   byte pair encoding over DPCM -- rdft2's sample
                             flash, the format its own 386 decompresses at
                             first boot (PLAN.md section 0)

      The ids live in rtl/spi_defs.vh and the decoders in rtl/spi_rom_decode.sv;
      `make check-mra` rejects a pair naming a part that does not exist or an id
      with no decoder behind it. For a decoded part the loader's part_size is
      the COMPRESSED length -- what arrives -- while the destination advances by
      what comes out. An example, for the rdft2 MRA this makes possible:

        <rom index="1">
          <part>01</part>       mod byte: SXX2C
          <part>0E 01</part>    part 14 is BPE+DPCM compressed
        </rom>

      IT MUST COME BEFORE THE index="0" ROM. Main_MiSTer sends the <rom>
      elements in file order, so an index-1 that follows the image arrives
      AFTER the loader has already walked its table -- the download then runs
      against the SXX2E layout and every part after the second lands wrong.
      That is not hypothetical: it is what this file did first, and the symptom
      was a 386 executing garbage at 0000:0400 with program lanes 2 and 3
      corrupt, lanes 0 and 1 intact, because those are the two the SXX2E table
      happens to place identically.
    -->
    <rom index="1"><part>01</part></rom>

    <rom index="0" address="0x30000000" zip="rdft.zip" md5="none">
        <!-- 386 program: four byte lanes, unlike rdfts's two bytes + one word -->
        <part name="raiden-fi_prg0_121196.u0211" crc="adcb5dbc"/>
        <part name="raiden-fi_prg1_121196.u0212" crc="60c5b92e"/>
        <part name="raiden-fi_prg2_121196.u0210" crc="44b86db5"/>
        <part name="raiden-fi_prg3_121196.u029"  crc="e70727ce"/>

        <!-- text layer: three byte lanes -->
        <part name="seibu_5.u0423" crc="8f8d4e14"/>
        <part name="seibu_6.u0424" crc="6ac64968"/>
        <part name="seibu_7.u048"  crc="4d87e1ea"/>

        <!-- background tiles -->
        <part name="gun_dogs_bg1-d.u0415" crc="6a68054c"/>
        <part name="gun_dogs_bg1-p.u0410" crc="3400794a"/>
        <part name="gun_dogs_bg2-d.u0424" crc="61cd2991"/>
        <part name="gun_dogs_bg2-p.u049"  crc="502d5799"/>

        <!-- sprites -->
        <part name="gun_dogs_obj-1.u0322" crc="59d86c99"/>
        <part name="gun_dogs_obj-2.u0324" crc="1ceb0b6f"/>
        <part name="gun_dogs_obj-3.u0323" crc="36e93234"/>

        <!--
          YMF271 sample flash, pre-programmed. 4 + 0x1A13B2 + 0x3828D + 0x269BD
          = 0x200000 exactly. The uncopied tail of both source ROMs is blank
          0xFF padding, verified, so nothing real is being left behind.
        -->
        <part>80 4A 4A 36</part>
        <part name="gun_dogs_pcm.u0217" crc="31253ad7" offset="0x000004" length="0x1A13B2"/>
        <part name="seibu_8.u0216"      crc="f88cb6e4" length="0x03828D"/>
        <part repeat="0x0269BD">FF</part>
    </rom>


    <!--
      Same control layout as rdfts; the names map to joystick bits 4 upwards IN
      ORDER and SeibuSPI.sv decodes them at exactly those positions.
    -->
    <buttons names="Shot,Bomb,Button 3,Start,Coin,Service Coin,Test"
             default="A,B,X,Start,Select,R,L" count="3"/>

    <switches default="00" base="0">
        <dip name="Flip Screen"  bits="0" ids="Off,On"/>
        <dip name="Service Mode" bits="1" ids="Off,On"/>
    </switches>
</misterromdescription>
